Kaela (Support): fyi raised by AcmePay — SSE streams to their webapp are getting truncated mid-stream. They see partial events then the client reconnects. Happens inconsistently, more often when responses take >15s of quiet time between events.
Kaela (Support): customer says this only happens when requests route through their Cloudflare Worker -> Cloudflare CDN -> our ALB origin. Direct calls to the Dedicated endpoint are fine.
Chris (eng-runtime): do we have a repro they sent? any example curl / browser steps?
Kaela (Support): yes sent a small repro curl and a node client. putting the curl they used:
```
curl -v -N "https://customer-proxy.example/stream?model=redwood-demo&session=xyz" \\
  -H "Accept: text/event-stream"
```
Kaela (Support): their node client uses EventSource polyfill and sees partial last event often missing closing \n\nDiego (eng-sre): classic proxy buffering / idle-timeout mismatch. Two quick suspects: (1) Cloudflare Worker middleware accidentally consumes the response (await res.text()) and thus buffers the whole body, or (2) Cloudflare/ALB idle timeouts + keepalive mismatch causing RST during quiet periods.
Diego (eng-sre): can we check Cloudflare edge logs for 524 (timeout) or any 502/522? also check origin socket RSTs.
Kaela (Support): customer attached Cloudflare edge logs — they show status 200 for most requests but a handful of 524-ish entries. Origin app logs at same time show last write succeeded then socket closed by peer.
Chris (eng-runtime): if the Worker uses something like `const r = await fetch(origin); const text = await r.text(); return new Response(text, {headers: {'Content-Type': 'text/event-stream'}})` that will buffer. streaming SSE requires returning the upstream body (or using a TransformStream) without awaiting the whole body.
Kaela (Support): they shared the Worker code, looks like exactly that pattern — `await response.text()` around line 42.
Maya (eng-platform): that explains it. If the Worker consumes upstream it will hold the full response — Cloudflare edge may then apply buffering behavior and close early if upstream stalls. also Workers have CPU/timeout constraints which can interact weirdly.
Diego (eng-sre): repro plan: 1) curl directly to origin -N and capture tcpdump on origin to see chunked writes and ACKs. 2) curl via the Worker host and observe timing and response headers. 3) request customer to run `curl -v --no-buffer` to ensure client isn't buffering.
Chris (eng-runtime): quick snippet to test streaming from a node client (useful to reproduce):
```
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
(async () => {
  const r = await fetch('https://edge.example/stream');
  r.body.setEncoding('utf8');
  r.body.on('data', chunk => console.log('chunk', chunk));
})();
```
Kaela (Support): tried their curl with `-v -N` and they see connection reset after ~18s of inactivity sometimes, other times after ~95s. inconsistent.
Diego (eng-sre): note: ALB default idle timeout = 60s. Cloudflare has a 100s time limit for some requests and also can buffer if Worker code reads body. If the Worker buffers, you'll see shorter time windows because the edge waits for the whole upstream.
Maya (eng-platform): immediate mitigation we can suggest to customer: 1) Hotfix their Worker to not call `res.text()` / `res.arrayBuffer()`; instead `return fetch(event.request)` or `return new Response(upstream.body, {headers: upstream.headers})` so streaming is preserved. Example correct Worker passthrough: 
```
addEventListener('fetch', event => {
  event.respondWith((async function() {
    const upstream = await fetch(event.request);
    return new Response(upstream.body, {headers: upstream.headers});
  })());
});
```
Maya (eng-platform): 2) Increase ALB idle timeout to >= 120s (customer currently at default 60s). 3) If they must use Cloudflare Workers, ensure the Worker is implemented as passthrough streaming.
Chris (eng-runtime): also ensure headers are right: upstream should send `Content-Type: text/event-stream`, `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` (no Content-Length), and avoid `Connection: close` mid-stream. If nginx sits in front of origin, set `proxy_buffering off; proxy_ignore_client_abort off;`
Diego (eng-sre): added: we saw deploy-bot show a Worker deploy yesterday at 2026-03-12T12:34Z with diff that introduced the `await res.text()` change. tagging deploy-bot logs here: deploy-bot: "worker deploy v2.1 — changed response handling"
deploy-bot: worker v2.1 applied at 2026-03-12T12:34Z (id: wb-331)
Diego (eng-sre): recommendation/action: 1) ask customer to revert Worker to streaming pattern or apply our example passthrough. 2) support guide to recommend ALB idle timeout >=120s for long-polling/SSE workloads and update our docs. 3) our side: add a runtime check that logs when an upstream is closed prematurely and annotate with X-Edge-Downstream-Note for support debugging.
Kaela (Support): I've pushed the Worker fix with them and they confirmed their engineer changed to passthrough. They also increased ALB idle to 180s.
Chris (eng-runtime): nice — any sign of re-occurrence after change?
Kaela (Support): customer reports clean streams for latest tests (20+ runs) using both browser and node client. No truncations. They left one test that still failed under very poor mobile network; looks like client-side reconnect logic handled it.
Maya (eng-platform): follow-ups: - Update support playbook with Cloudflare Worker buffering note and sample passthrough snippet. - Add a small diagnostic FAQ: "Are you calling res.text() in edge middleware?"
Diego (eng-sre): I'll add an alert in edge logs to surface `worker_body_buffered=true` when we see a worker response size exceed 0 but upstream writes happened earlier in timeline.
Kaela (Support): closing with customer — will keep on watch. tagging @Chris and @Maya for docs/telemetry tasks. :thumbsup:
Chris (eng-runtime): great, thanks.
Maya (eng-platform): thanks team — rolling into next-week platform notes.
Kaela (Support): closing thread — will reopen if customer sees regression.
